  "diagnosis_text": "`h1 \u2192 h5` is failing completely: 3/3 ICMP probes were lost. Both endpoint access segments are healthy: `h1` reaches local peer `h2`, and `h5` reaches `h6`; all relevant ports and all six inter-switch links are reported enabled/ACTIVE.\n\nThe intended path is:\n\n`h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n\nThe fault is localized to **s1\u2019s forwarding rule for traffic from h1 to h5**. On `s1`:\n\n- Port 1 leads back toward `s2`\n- Port 2 leads toward `s3`, which is the required direction for `h5`\n\nHowever, a priority-50000 rule installed by `org.coms6200.experiment` matches:\n\n- source MAC `00:00:00:00:00:01` (`h1`)\n- destination MAC `00:00:00:00:00:05` (`h5`)\n\nand outputs it on **port 1**, back to `s2`. It has already matched 7 packets. This overrides the normal baseline destination rule for h5, which correctly selects **port 2**.\n\n**Diagnosis:** an erroneous high-priority, flow-specific rule on **s1** misroutes `h1 \u2192 h5` frames toward `s2` instead of `s3`, creating a loop/wrong-path condition. The corrective action is to remove or amend that override so this traffic exits `s1` port 2.",
